.TH chtone 1 "Mar 2014" "plc-utils-2.1.6" "Qualcomm Atheros Powerline Toolkit"

.SH NAME
chtone - Qualcomm Atheros QCA7500 Tone Map Monitor

.SH SYNOPSIS
.BR chtone
.RI [ options ] 
.IR node 
.IR peer 
.RI [> stdout ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Read QCA7500 TX tone map using VS_TONE_MAP_CHAR version 1, or VS_RX_TONE_MAP_CHAR version 1, and print them on stdout.
With VS_RX_TONE_MAP_CHAR, AGC is included in the output.
See option \fB-r\fR for more information.

.PP
Powerline devices cannot compute tone map values unless data has passed through them, either to or from the powerline.
In most cases, the \fBping\fR utility will generate enough traffic to create a usable tone map.
To get a responsive tonemap, one must send more traffic at higher data rates, possibly using \fBttcp\fR or \fBiperf\fR or similar utility.

.PP
It is important to get the direction correct or the program may timeout.
The direction is a function of the node and peer device and option \fB-r\fR.
By default, data flow is from node to peer and the tonemaps are transmit tone maps.
WIth option \fB-r\fR, data flow is from peer to node and the tonemaps are receive tone maps.

.PP
This program is part of the Qualcomm Atheros Powerline Toolkit.
See the \fBplc\fR man page for an overview and installation instructions.
MIMO powerline communications technology is new with the QCA7500.
It is explained in the HomePlug AV 2.1 specification.

.SH COMMENTS
This program is similar to legacy programs \fBint6ktone\fR, \fBamptone\fR and \fBplctone\fR but supports \fBSISO\fR and \fBMINO\fR transmission modes.
Older firmware versions may not recognize options supported by the program.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
-\fBc\fI coupling\fR
Tonemap coupling.
Use the number \fB0\fR or the string "\fBpri\fR" for SISO primary coupling.
Use the number \fB1\fR or the string "\fBalt\fR" for SISO alternate coupling.
Use the number \fB0\fR or the string "\fBmimo\fR" for MIMO coupling.
This program will accept any numeric value from \fB0\fR through \fB255\fR but runtime firmware will reject out-of-range values.
The default value is \fB0\fR.

.TP
.RB - e
Redirects stderr messages to stdout.
By convention status and error messages are printed on stderr while primary program output is printed on stdout.
This option prints all output on stdout in cases where this is desired.

.TP
-\fBi\fI interface\fR
Select the host Ethernet interface.
All requests are sent via this host interface and only reponses received via this host interface are recognized.
The default interface is \fBeth1\fR because most people use \fBeth0\fR as their principle network connection; however, if environment string "PLC" is defined then it takes precedence over the default interface.
This option then takes precedence over either default.

.TP
.RB - q
Enter quiet mode.
Progress messages are suppressed.

.TP
.RB -r
Read and display receive tone maps with VS_RX_TONE_MAP_CHAR.
Output is identical to transmit tone maps but AGC values are printed ahead of the tone map data.

.TP
.RB - v 
Enter verbose mode.
All Etherenet frames sent or received by the program are displayed on stdout.

.TP
-\fB?\fR, --\fBhelp\fR
Print program help summary on stdout.
This option takes precedence over other options on the command line.

.TP
-\fB!\fR, --\fBversion\fR
Print program version information on stdout.
This option takes precedence over other options on the command line.
Use this option when sending screen dumps to Atheros Technical Support so that they know exactly which version of the Linux Toolkit you are using.

.SH ARGUMENTS

.TP
.BR node
The MAC address of the reporting device.

.TP
.BR peer
The MAC address of the peer device.

.SH REFERENCES
See the Qualcomm Atheros HomePlug AV Firmware Technical Reference Manual for more information.

